Almost everything that a computer processes, stores, transmits or displays can be represented in binary (0s and 1s).

For computer science and offensive security, you should understand the following major categories of binary representation.

### 1\. Number systems

Fundamental

| Number type           | Example | Binary representation           |
| --------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------- |
| Decimal integer       | 12      | 1100                            |
| Negative integer      | -5      | Two's complement representation |
| Binary number         | 1100    | 1100                            |
| Octal number          | 14      | 1100                            |
| Hexadecimal number    | C       | 1100                            |
| Floating-point number | 12.625  | 1100.101                        |

Python:

```python
print(bin(12))
print(format(12, "08b"))
```

### 2\. Characters and text

Letters, numbers, punctuation and symbols can be encoded into binary.

| Character | Encoding | Binary   |
| --------- | -------- | -------- |
| A         | ASCII    | 01000001 |
| a         | ASCII    | 01100001 |
| B         | ASCII    | 01000010 |
| 0         | ASCII    | 00110000 |
| Space     | ASCII    | 00100000 |
| !         | ASCII    | 00100001 |

Other text encodings include:

* ASCII

* UTF-8

* UTF-16

* UTF-32

* Unicode code points

Python:

```python
text = "ABC"

for char in text:
    print(char, format(ord(char), "08b"))
```

### 3\. Boolean values and logic

Computers use binary representations of logical states.

| Logical value | Binary | |---|---| | False | 0 | | True | 1 | | AND | `&` | | OR | `|` | | NOT | `~` | | XOR | `^` |

Other Boolean operations include NAND, NOR and XNOR.

